WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.478
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.528 --> 00:00:03.908
This video is about the first hours of an infection.

3
00:00:03.958 --> 00:00:08.063
The exam asks you to compare innate and adaptive immunity, name

4
00:00:08.113 --> 00:00:11.955
the barrier molecules, and place each phase on a time line.

5
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:16.520
Innate immunity is fully functional before an infection occurs.

6
00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.941
It gives continuous barrier defense.

7
00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:24.590
It reacts to microbes in the first hours or days, before adaptive responses develop.

8
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:28.030
Adaptive immunity needs exposure to the microbe.

9
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.921
Lymphocyte activation and differentiation take several days.

10
00:00:31.971 --> 00:00:36.655
So the adaptive response is slow, and innate immunity must protect first.

11
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:40.336
Now let's compare the two branches side by side.

12
00:00:40.386 --> 00:00:41.663
First, specificity.

13
00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:45.575
Innate immunity recognizes molecules shared by groups of

14
00:00:45.625 --> 00:00:49.486
related microbes, and molecules from damaged host cells.

15
00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:52.350
That is only about one thousand products.

16
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.052
Adaptive immunity can recognize millions of antigens.

17
00:00:56.433 --> 00:00:57.565
Next, receptors.

18
00:00:57.615 --> 00:01:01.112
Innate immunity uses germline-encoded, invariant

19
00:01:01.162 --> 00:01:05.102
receptors, and there are fewer than one hundred types.

20
00:01:05.152 --> 00:01:08.057
Adaptive immunity uses only two receptor

21
00:01:08.107 --> 00:01:11.456
types, immunoglobulin and the T-cell receptor.

22
00:01:11.506 --> 00:01:16.184
Each has millions of variants from somatically rearranged genes.

23
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.641
Innate receptors are nonclonal.

24
00:01:18.691 --> 00:01:22.066
All cells of one lineage carry the same receptors.

25
00:01:22.116 --> 00:01:24.122
Adaptive receptors are clonal.

26
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.808
Innate immunity has limited memory.

27
00:01:26.858 --> 00:01:30.177
A repeat exposure usually gives the same kinetics,

28
00:01:30.227 --> 00:01:33.141
magnitude and quality as the first response.

29
00:01:33.191 --> 00:01:35.095
Adaptive immunity has memory.

30
00:01:35.145 --> 00:01:39.137
Secondary adaptive responses are usually faster and greater.

31
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.586
Neither branch normally reacts against self.

32
00:01:42.636 --> 00:01:46.577
Innate immunity ignores healthy cells for three reasons.

33
00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:50.925
Normal cells lack most ligands, the receptors sit in separate

34
00:01:50.975 --> 00:01:54.845
compartments, and regulatory proteins block activation.

35
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.960
So what does innate immunity actually see?

36
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.305
Molecules shared by classes of microbes are called

37
00:02:01.355 --> 00:02:04.584
pathogen-associated molecular patterns, or PAMPs.

38
00:02:04.634 --> 00:02:07.795
They are often essential for microbial survival.

39
00:02:07.845 --> 00:02:12.077
So microbes usually cannot evade innate immunity by losing them.

40
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:18.177
Damaged or dying host cells release damage-associated molecular patterns, or DAMPs.

41
00:02:18.227 --> 00:02:22.226
DAMPs also signal sterile injury, such as burns or trauma.

42
00:02:22.276 --> 00:02:26.344
Cells that die by apoptosis generally do not release DAMPs.

43
00:02:26.394 --> 00:02:29.136
Some DAMP cytokines are called alarmins.

44
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.005
Now to the front line.

45
00:02:31.055 --> 00:02:35.316
The main interfaces with the environment are the skin and the

46
00:02:35.366 --> 00:02:39.485
mucosa of the gut, the airways and the genitourinary tract.

47
00:02:39.535 --> 00:02:42.736
Surface epithelia give three types of barrier:

48
00:02:42.786 --> 00:02:45.634
mechanical, chemical and microbiological.

49
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:47.536
Think of it like a castle.

50
00:02:47.586 --> 00:02:49.549
The wall is the physical barrier.

51
00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:52.600
Boiling oil from the wall is the chemical barrier.

52
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.016
Friendly tenants who fill every room are the microbiota.

53
00:02:56.367 --> 00:02:58.177
First, the physical barrier.

54
00:02:58.227 --> 00:03:03.227
Epithelial cells form tight junctions that block microbes between the cells.

55
00:03:03.277 --> 00:03:06.948
In the skin, the outer keratin layer blocks penetration.

56
00:03:06.998 --> 00:03:10.470
Skin infections usually occur after a barrier breach.

57
00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:14.186
Mucosal epithelia produce mucus, which contains

58
00:03:14.236 --> 00:03:16.937
mucins and impairs microbial invasion.

59
00:03:16.987 --> 00:03:22.584
In the airways, goblet cells secrete mucus, and beating cilia move it outward.

60
00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:26.131
In the gut, peristalsis helps to remove microbes.

61
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.192
Here is the clinical link.

62
00:03:28.242 --> 00:03:31.072
In cystic fibrosis, thick mucus or impaired

63
00:03:31.122 --> 00:03:33.885
ciliary movement leads to lung infections.

64
00:03:33.935 --> 00:03:38.239
The bacteria colonize the epithelial surface but do not cross it.

65
00:03:38.289 --> 00:03:43.598
Failure of peristalsis typically leads to bacterial overgrowth in the gut lumen.

66
00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:45.988
Second, the chemical barrier.

67
00:03:46.038 --> 00:03:51.559
In the upper gut, stomach acid, digestive enzymes and bile salts act together.

68
00:03:51.609 --> 00:03:56.059
Tears and saliva contain lysozyme, which cleaves peptidoglycan.

69
00:03:56.109 --> 00:04:00.630
Lysozyme works better against Gram-positive bacteria, because an

70
00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.416
LPS outer layer covers the peptidoglycan of Gram-negative bacteria.

71
00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:09.651
Mammals have three important classes of antimicrobial

72
00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:13.289
peptides: defensins, cathelicidins and histatins.

73
00:04:13.339 --> 00:04:16.110
Defensins are small cationic peptides.

74
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.451
Within minutes, they disrupt the membranes of

75
00:04:19.501 --> 00:04:22.644
bacteria, fungi and some enveloped viruses.

76
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.861
Paneth cells in the small-bowel crypts are a major source of alpha-defensins.

77
00:04:28.911 --> 00:04:34.849
Beta-defensins come mainly from the airway, urogenital tract, skin and tongue.

78
00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:39.301
Cathelicidin gives LL-37, which binds and neutralizes LPS.

79
00:04:39.351 --> 00:04:44.214
Histatins from the salivary glands act against Candida albicans.

80
00:04:44.567 --> 00:04:46.884
Third, the microbiological barrier.

81
00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:52.093
Commensal microbes compete with pathogens for nutrients and attachment sites.

82
00:04:52.143 --> 00:04:57.641
They make antimicrobial substances, such as lactic acid from vaginal lactobacilli.

83
00:04:57.691 --> 00:05:02.241
They also stimulate epithelial cells to make antimicrobial peptides.

84
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.444
When antibiotics kill commensals, pathogens

85
00:05:05.494 --> 00:05:08.270
frequently replace them and cause disease.

86
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.434
And beneath all epithelial surfaces, phagocytes

87
00:05:11.484 --> 00:05:14.462
wait to engulf any microbe that gets through.

88
00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:17.663
Now let's put everything on a time line.

89
00:05:17.713 --> 00:05:21.119
The response to a new pathogen has three phases.

90
00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:25.798
Phase one is the immediate innate phase, from zero to four hours.

91
00:05:25.848 --> 00:05:31.269
Preformed soluble molecules act at once: lysozyme, defensins and complement.

92
00:05:31.633 --> 00:05:36.328
Phase two is the early induced innate phase, from four to ninety-six hours.

93
00:05:36.378 --> 00:05:39.492
Innate cells recognize PAMPs and become activated.

94
00:05:39.542 --> 00:05:44.110
An inflammatory response often first counters the spread of the pathogen.

95
00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:49.783
During this phase, dendritic cells carry antigen to local lymphoid tissue.

96
00:05:49.833 --> 00:05:54.352
Phase three, the adaptive phase, starts after ninety-six hours.

97
00:05:54.402 --> 00:05:59.719
It is late because rare specific B and T cells must first expand clonally.

98
00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:02.437
Innate defenses act within minutes.

99
00:06:02.487 --> 00:06:05.847
An adaptive response is necessary only when innate

100
00:06:05.897 --> 00:06:08.916
defenses are bypassed, evaded or overwhelmed.

101
00:06:08.966 --> 00:06:12.939
Even then, innate immunity keeps pathogen numbers in check.

102
00:06:12.989 --> 00:06:17.372
The phase boundaries are approximate and vary between infections.

103
00:06:17.733 --> 00:06:19.665
The two branches work together.

104
00:06:19.715 --> 00:06:24.587
The innate response gives early danger signals that start adaptive responses.

105
00:06:24.637 --> 00:06:28.805
In turn, adaptive responses often make innate mechanisms stronger.

106
00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:30.924
Five things to remember.

107
00:06:30.974 --> 00:06:37.024
One: innate immunity is fully functional before infection and has limited memory.

108
00:06:37.074 --> 00:06:42.973
Two: it uses fewer than one hundred germline-encoded, nonclonal receptor types.

109
00:06:43.023 --> 00:06:48.395
Three: epithelia give mechanical, chemical and microbiological barriers.

110
00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:55.104
Four: key barrier molecules are lysozyme, defensins, cathelicidin and histatins.

111
00:06:55.154 --> 00:06:59.176
Five: the three phases are zero to four hours, four

112
00:06:59.226 --> 00:07:03.009
to ninety-six hours, and after ninety-six hours.

113
00:07:03.367 --> 00:07:04.604
And one exam trap.

114
00:07:04.654 --> 00:07:08.038
Lysozyme is more effective against Gram-positive

115
00:07:08.088 --> 00:07:10.684
bacteria, not Gram-negative bacteria.

116
00:07:10.734 --> 00:07:15.763
The LPS outer layer of Gram-negative bacteria covers the peptidoglycan.

117
00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:19.424
Next: the cells of innate immunity.

118
00:07:19.474 --> 00:07:24.197
We meet the phagocytes, dendritic cells, NK cells,

119
00:07:24.247 --> 00:07:28.970
mast cells, eosinophils and innate lymphoid cells.
